            "logprobs": [],
            "text": "{\"strengths\":[\"Defines PED precisely using percentage changes and correctly notes the usual use of absolute values.\",\"Explains two distinct determinants—close substitutes and time for adjustment—with clear causal links to greater or lower responsiveness of quantity demanded.\",\"Uses well-chosen illustrative contexts (cereal, essential medicine and petrol) to make the mechanisms concrete and maintains relevant qualifications.\"],\"improvements\":[],\"mistakes\":[],\"examinerComment\":\"Estimated practice mark: an excellent, fully focused response. Both required determinants are identified and developed through accurate causal analysis of how consumers can adjust quantity demanded after a price change. The qualification that more adjustment time need not make PED greater than one is particularly precise. A diagram was not needed for this explanation task.\",\"studyNext\":\"Optional extension (not needed for credit): practise explaining further PED determinants, such as proportion of income spent and addiction/habit formation, with the same explicit link to consumers’ ability and willingness to switch or reduce consumption.\",\"assessmentFormat\":\"paper_1_a\",\"paper\":\"paper_1\",\"questionPart\":\"a\",\"levelRelevance\":\"shared_sl_hl\",\"assessmentSkills\":[\"definition\",\"economic_analysis\",\"application\"],\"commandTerm\":\"explain\",\"commandTermLabel\":\"Explain\",\"syllabusUnit\":\"unit_2\",\"syllabusTopic\":\"2.5\",\"topicLabel\":\"Elasticity of demand\",\"classificationConfidence\":\"high\",\"markingConfidence\":\"high\",\"diagramExpected\":false,\"diagramSubmitted\":false,\"diagramAssessmentStatus\":\"not_relevant\",\"workingsExpected\":false,\"workingsSubmitted\":false,\"workingsAssessmentStatus\":\"not_relevant\",\"attachmentContent\":\"none\",\"assessableEarned\":10,\"markBreakdown\":[{\"label\":\"Knowledge and terminology\",\"awarded\":4,\"available\":4,\"reason\":\"PED is accurately defined and key terms including quantity demanded, substitutes, elastic and inelastic are used precisely.\"},{\"label\":\"Economic analysis\",\"awarded\":4,\"available\":4,\"reason\":\"For each determinant, the answer clearly traces how a price rise changes consumers’ scope to adjust and therefore the magnitude of the quantity response.\"},{\"label\":\"Application to context\",\"awarded\":4,\"available\":4,\"reason\":\"The cereal, medicine and petrol contexts are relevant illustrative applications that directly clarify the mechanisms.\"},{\"label\":\"Structure and clarity\",\"awarded\":4,\"available\":4,\"reason\":\"The response is coherent, separates the two determinants clearly and remains tightly focused on the question.\"}],\"bandRationale\":\"Best fit: 9–10, upper. The response gives accurate, well-developed and coherent explanations of exactly two PED determinants, with explicit causal reasoning and relevant illustrations. It also includes a valid qualification about long-run elasticity without losing focus.\",\"limitations\":[],\"examinerJudgment\":{\"task\":\"Explain two determinants of price elasticity of demand.\",\"demonstrated\":\"Accurate definition of PED and fully developed explanations of the availability of close substitutes and time for adjustment, explicitly linking each to the responsiveness of quantity demanded after a price change.\",\"materialLimitations\":[],\"selectedBand\":\"9-10\",\"withinBand\":\"upper\",\"diagramEffect\":\"not_required\",\"summary\":\"Fully focused, accurate and coherent analysis meets the highest band to a great extent.\"},\"componentEvaluation\":null}"
          }
